{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1785260962,"nonce":"Ol9Hrb9btX8qYt9i6dXEWcB+CC8=","title":"ADR-0065 s2: RetentionProfile — operator-defined, author-selectable retention","message":"The right retention mix differs per organisation (JS is dominated by `node_modules`, Rust by `target/`), so retention must be tunable — and tunable by the **operator**, not the author. ADR-0061's governing principle forbids pushing substrate knowledge into authored YAML: \"where the substrate is expensive, the system pays, not the author.\"\n\nCopies **ADR-0055's `PlacementProfile` pattern exactly**: an operator-owned, cluster-scoped named bundle in gitops. A Pipeline may **name** a profile; it never defines the values, so no byte cost enters authored YAML.\n\nTwo of these profile types is the point at which the pattern should be **shared rather than duplicated** from ADR-0055.\n\n- [ ] Named bundle carrying: the Depot space budget, per-class TTLs, Cache-eligible directories, and the drop-and-re-derive thresholds\n- [ ] Author may name one; may not define values\n- [ ] Composes with ADR-0061's manual **pin** (\"keep this Run's workspaces\") as the per-Run investigation escape hatch\n- [ ] The profile-resolution machinery is factored out of ADR-0055 rather than copy-pasted","files":null}]}